<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Engelbert Dollfuss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Engelbert Dollfuss was an Austrian Christian Social politician, chancellor from 1932. In March 1933 he suspended parliament and ruled by decree, banning the Communists and Austrian Nazis. After crushing the Social Democrats in the civil war of February 1934, he created the authoritarian Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He was murdered by Austrian Nazis during a failed coup in July 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss.jpg" alt="Engelbert Dollfuss" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engelbert_Dollfuss_1-E-652.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Engelbert Dollfuss was born on 4 October 1892 into a poor peasant family near Texingtal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, and local parish priests helped to pay for his schooling. He enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> to study theology but changed to law in 1912. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he was rejected as too short but insisted and was accepted, serving 37 months on the Italian Front with the Tyrolese militia and rising to first lieutenant. After the war he worked for the Lower Austrian Peasants' Union and became an unofficial leader of the Austrian peasantry. He was president of the Federal Railways from 1930 and Federal Minister of Agriculture and Forestry from March 1931.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 10 May 1932 President Wilhelm Miklas offered him the chancellorship, and he took office on 20 May at the head of a coalition with a majority of one vote. In March 1933 the president of the National Council, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-renner">Karl Renner</a>, and his two vice presidents resigned in a dispute over voting procedure. Dollfuss declared the council unworkable, and from 7 March he governed by emergency decree under a wartime law of 1917. When the council tried to reconvene on 15 March, police barred the chamber. He banned the Communist Party on 26 May 1933 and the Austrian Nazi Party on 19 June, and in September 1933 formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-austria">Fatherland Front</a>, modelled on Catholic corporatist ideals and Italian fascism.</p><p>On 12 February 1934 armed resistance by the Linz Social Democrats to a search for weapons sparked the Austrian Civil War. Police and army used artillery, and the rising collapsed by 15 February with about 350 deaths. The Social Democrats were outlawed. A new constitution took effect on 1 May 1934, creating the Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dollfuss opposed German demands for the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> guaranteed Austrian independence in August 1933. On 25 July 1934 a group of Austrian Nazis, including Otto Planetta and Franz Holzweber, entered the Chancellery in a coup attempt and shot him. The conspirators surrendered after the military threatened to blow up the building, and Planetta and Holzweber were hanged on 31 July. Mussolini mobilised troops on the Austrian border. About 500,000 people attended Dollfuss's burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Kurt Schuschnigg</a> kept the regime until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of 1938.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Engelbert Dollfuss” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…from the German-speaking lands of the fallen Habsburg monarchy and renamed by the Treaty of Saint-Germain, lasted until <b>Engelbert Dollfuss</b> shut down parliament in 1933; the Austrian Civil War of February 1934 outlawed the Social Democrats, and on 13 March 1938 the Anschluss made Austria a part of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Chancellor Engelbert Dollfuss</b> suspended parliament in March 1933 and ruled by decree, banning the Communists and Austrian Nazis.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-austria">Federal State of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1933 <b>Chancellor Engelbert Dollfuss</b> suspended parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-austrian-republic">First Austrian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Texing, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.05,15.32&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Texing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4907 482 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Engelbert Dollfuss"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5357 752h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5357 752h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.05,15.32&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
